  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThrough midweek...\n\nAn upper-level ridge across the central portion of the country and\ndownstream low pressure/troughing over the East Coast associated\nwith a departing major Nor'Easter event keeps our forecast area \nbeneath northwest flow aloft. This should keep the area dry headed\ninto midweek and allow for daytime highs to nudge over the 90 \ndegree mark Tuesday through Thursday. By Thursday and especially \ninto the weekend, humidity will be noticeably on the increase. As\nan area of low pressure in the Southwest US interacts with the \nremnants of current East Pacific Hurricane Polo, the merging\nsystem will slowly move closer to our area. This low will \nreinforce a seasonably robust cold frontal boundary to our \nNorthwest and to the south and east of this developing activity, \nwinds will begin to shift southerly, transporting, rich, moist \nGulf air northward. Dewpoints begin the week in the 50s F but \nlikely rise to near 70 F into the weekend. In the meantime, full \nsun in the afternoons will support mixing and drying with drought \nconditions still present. For this reason, the \"Limited\" fire \nweather risk graphic was maintained for northern portions of the \narea through early this week. \n\nLate week into the weekend....\n\nWhile guidance has trended wetter overall over the next 7-10 days,\nmostly due to the GFS coming around to more of the signal shown\nin the Euro guidance, modeling continues to struggle both with the\ncomplex interaction of the baroclinic low and any tropical\nresiduals but also with the intensity of the subtropical ridge\nthat has been our hot, fair weather companion since Spring\nabandoned us. This results in higher than typical uncertainty in\nexact frontal progress through the weekend and perhaps even into\nnext week. That said, there is growing confidence that increased  \nPWAT values (likely at some point in the 5-8 day range exceeding \n2 inches) coupled with some improvement in deep layer flow amid \nincreasing upper southwesterly influence will likely result in at \nleast some risk of widespread, potentially at times heavy, \nrainfall. It remains too early to give more than very broad \nestimates of rainfall totals for the area, but current 50th \npercentile estimates suggest an inch or two could be common. \nShould some of the more aggressive members of the model ensemble \nsuite prove more accurate, some risk for flooding in some places \ncan't be ruled out. For now, while thunder would be likely at \ntimes, the system seems unlikely to pair adequate instability with\nsufficiently high deep layer shear to a degree necessary to pose \na substantial threat for organized severe weather. \n\nWe certainly could use a healthy rain as most of the area boasts \na 2 to 4 inch rainfall deficit for the last 30 days with dry \nconditions preceding. October is often the driest month for our \narea and this event may present one of the better opportunities \nfor widespread rain. Trends for hazardous weather possibilities associated\nwith this activity will be monitored over the coming days. /86/NF/",
